So the cleanest Java way to reverse a String is using StringBuilder.reverse():
```java
String reversed = new StringBuilder(str).reverse().toString();
```
That's the one-liner they expect you to know.

If the interviewer asks for a manual implementation (to test your logic), iterate from the last index down to 0 and append each character:
```java
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
for (int i = str.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    sb.append(str.charAt(i));
}
return sb.toString();
```

You can't reverse a String in place because Strings are immutable. If they ask for a character-level solution, convert to char array: `char[] chars = str.toCharArray()`, use two pointers to swap characters, then `new String(chars)`.

Edge cases to mention: empty string → returns empty, single character → returns same, palindrome check (compare reversed with original).

So counting character frequencies is a fundamental string problem. There are two approaches depending on the input:

For lowercase English letters only, use an int array of size 26: `int[] freq = new int[26]`. For each character c, do `freq[c - 'a']++`. To print, loop through 0-25 and output `(char)('a' + i)` for each non-zero count. This is O(1) space (bounded by alphabet) and very fast.

For arbitrary characters (uppercase, digits, Unicode), use a HashMap:
```java
Map<Character, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
    freq.put(c, freq.getOrDefault(c, 0) + 1);
}
```

Or cleaner: `freq.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum)` — adds 1 if key doesn't exist, or sums if it does.

The follow-up question is often 'find first non-repeating character' — build the frequency map, then iterate the original string again and return the first character with count 1.

So two strings are anagrams if they contain the same characters with the same frequencies, just in different order. 'listen' and 'silent' are anagrams.

Approach 1 — Sort and compare: Convert both to char arrays, sort both, convert back to String (or compare char by char), return if equal. O(n log n) time, O(n) space. Clean and readable.
```java
char[] a = s1.toCharArray(); Arrays.sort(a);
char[] b = s2.toCharArray(); Arrays.sort(b);
return Arrays.equals(a, b);
```

Approach 2 — Frequency array: Use an int[26] array. Increment for chars in s1, decrement for chars in s2. If all zeros at the end, they're anagrams. O(n) time, O(1) extra space. Better for large strings.

Always start with the length check: `if (s1.length() != s2.length()) return false`. Clarify case sensitivity with the interviewer — usually normalize to lowercase first.

Two-pass approach:

Pass 1: count frequencies
```java
Map<Character, Integer> freq = new LinkedHashMap<>();
for (char c : s.toCharArray())
    freq.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
```

Pass 2: find first with count 1
```java
for (char c : s.toCharArray())
    if (freq.get(c) == 1) return c;
return '\0';
```

For lowercase only: use int[26] — increment for pass 1, scan for count==1 in pass 2.
Time: O(n). Space: O(k) where k = distinct chars.
